    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.Intro">A custom rule is an alert of your own. It looks for a text in the lines EVE writes to its game log or to the log of a chat channel, and raises an alert like the built-in ones: the border, the badge, a toast, a sound and a line in the alert history.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.rules.Title">Rules</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.rules.Body">**Add rule** makes an empty rule. The three examples make a rule you can change.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A rule works while its switch is on. A rule that can't run says why, and can't be switched on.&#x0A;- Its alert has the rule's name, and the colour and the toast of its severity.&#x0A;- **Quiet time** is how long the rule stays quiet for one character after it fired. Empty means the quiet time every alert has.&#x0A;- There can be 50 rules.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.match.Title">What a rule looks at</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.match.Body">**Text** finds the words anywhere in a line, in capital or small letters. **Regular expression** is a .NET pattern.&#x0A;&#x0A;- From a line of the game log a rule sees the text without its time and without colour and font codes, for example: (combat) 123 from Guristas Despoiler - Hits&#x0A;- From a chat line it sees: Name &gt; message&#x0A;- A regular expression gets a limited time for each line. A match that takes too long is given up, and once the regular expressions have used up a line's time, the rest of them skip that line.&#x0A;- A rule that costs too much is switched off by MultiPreview: its pattern takes far too long on some lines, or your regular-expression rules together slow down the reading of the logs (on every line, or only now and then) and this rule is among the slowest. Its card and error_log.txt say so. It is tried again when you change any rule or restart MultiPreview. A rule that looks for a plain text is never switched off or skipped.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.chat.Title">Chat channels</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.chat.Body">A chat rule reads the log EVE writes for a channel. **Channel** is the first part of that log file's name: Fleet, Corp, Local, or the name of a channel you joined. Open the list to see the channels your chat log folder has logs of.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Chat logging must be on in EVE, and **Read chat logs** in MultiPreview (Alerts › Events).&#x0A;- The alert is for the character whose client wrote the log. **Characters** keeps a rule to some of them.&#x0A;- **Ignore my own messages** skips what that character wrote.&#x0A;- Rules read ten channels at most, beside Local.&#x0A;- A channel is read while a rule that is switched on names it, and only what is said from then on, while MultiPreview runs, is looked at.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.test.Title">Try a line</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.test.Body">Paste a line and press **Test**. The answer comes from the code that reads the logs, so a rule that matches here matches in the game.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Copy a line from a log file, or type what the game showed.&#x0A;- Under the answer is the text the pattern was tried on.&#x0A;- The time is how long the match took. When a regular expression took so long that its rule would be switched off, a line under the answer says so.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.file.Title">Where the rules are</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.custom.file.Body">The rules are in custom_alerts.json in the settings folder. They are not part of a profile, and nothing is sent anywhere.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A rules file MultiPreview can't read is set aside under a new name in the same folder, and MultiPreview starts without rules.&#x0A;- A rule's own sound must be a file on a drive of this PC. If that file is no longer there, the alert plays the default sound.&#x0A;- What a rule matched is kept in the alert history with the alert, cut to 160 characters: a line of the game log, which can name other players, or what another player wrote in a chat channel. Clear the history, or turn it off (Alerts › Alert hub), if you don't want that.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Hist.Off">The alert history is switched off, so new alerts are not kept. Switch it on under Alerts › Alert hub.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Hist.CountRows">Alerts: {0} in {1} rows</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Rules.Off.timeout">MultiPreview switched this rule off: its pattern took far too long on some lines, and other alerts had to wait for it. A repeat inside a repeat is the usual cause. The rule is tried again when you change any of your rules or restart MultiPreview.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Rules.Off.average">MultiPreview switched this rule off: your regular-expression rules were slowing down the reading of the logs, and this one was the slowest or one of the slow ones. A simpler pattern, or plain text, is faster. The rule is tried again when you change any of your rules or restart MultiPreview.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Rules.Off.peaks">MultiPreview switched this rule off: now and then it took long on a line, and together with your other rules that held up the reading of the logs. A very busy PC can cause this too. The rule is tried again when you change any of your rules or restart MultiPreview.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Rules.NoChatLogs">No chat logs found in your chat log folder. Switch on chat logging in EVE, or type the channel's name.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Rules.Test.Slow">Slow. A rule whose regular expression takes more than {0} ms on most lines gets switched off. If other lines take this long too, make the pattern simpler.</sys:String>
    <!-- Ideas batch 5 (Plan 9) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.clients.hotkeys.jump">Jumping to a client</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.backToPrevious">Back to the client before this one</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.backToPrevious.Desc">Switches to the client you used before this one. Press it again to come back.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Switch.NoPrevious">No other client to go back to</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.back.Title">Back to the client before</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.back.Body">**Back to the client before this one** takes you to the client you used before the one in front, and a second press brings you back: main and scout, or main and the logi you just checked.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A client counts as used once you stay on it for half a second. Clients you pass over faster than that, as with a held cycle key, don't count.&#x0A;- A client that has closed is skipped.&#x0A;- From another program, the key goes to the client you used last; a keyboard key works there only while **Hotkeys work** is set to **Everywhere**.&#x0A;- The key is not set until you set one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.lastAlert">Go to the last alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.lastAlert.Desc">Switches to the client with the most severe alert you have not looked at yet. Press it again for the next one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Switch.NoAlert">No alert to go to</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Switch.NoEarlierAlert">No earlier alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.lastAlert.Title">Go to the last alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.lastAlert.Body">**Go to the last alert** takes you to the client that needs you, without looking for the thumbnail that flashes.&#x0A;&#x0A;- While there are alerts you have not looked at, it goes to the most severe one first: critical before warning before info, and the newest first among equals. Looking at a client clears its alerts, so the next press goes to the next client.&#x0A;- With nothing left, it walks back through the alerts of this session, newest first, one client per press. After 30 seconds without a press it starts at the newest again. The walk back reads the alert history, so it needs **Keep an alert history** switched on.&#x0A;- When the newest alert is on the client in front and less than 30 seconds old, the key stays on that client and says so near the cursor: it is the client that needs you. Press again to walk on; if another alert has come in on that client in the meantime, the key stays once more. This reads the alert history too, so it also needs **Keep an alert history** switched on.&#x0A;- A character whose client has closed is skipped, and so is an alert that was muted or snoozed when it came in.&#x0A;- With nothing to go to, a short note appears near the cursor. No sound: it could be taken for an alert.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.cycleSameSystem">Cycle only within this solar system</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.cycleSameSystem.Desc">The cycle keys skip clients that are in another solar system than the client in front. When no other client matches, the keys go through all clients and say so.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.cycleAlerting">Cycle only through clients with an alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.cycleAlerting.Desc">The cycle keys go only to clients with an alert you have not looked at yet. When no other client matches, the keys go through all clients and say so.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.cycleSkipMuted">Skip clients whose alerts are muted</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.cycleSkipMuted.Desc">The cycle keys pass over a character you muted from its thumbnail's menu. When no other client matches, the keys go through all clients and say so.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Switch.CycleFilterEmpty">No other client matches the cycle filter: switching through all of them</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.cycleFilters.Title">Cycle filters</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.cycleFilters.Body">Three switches narrow where the cycle keys go: the two keys for all running clients, every group's keys, and a key shared by several characters. All three are off until you turn one on.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Cycle only within this solar system** keeps the keys among the clients in the same solar system as the client in front. A client whose system MultiPreview doesn't know yet is not skipped.&#x0A;- **Cycle only through clients with an alert** goes only to clients with an alert you have not looked at. In a fight, that is the clients that need you.&#x0A;- **Skip clients whose alerts are muted** passes over characters whose alerts you muted (right-click a thumbnail).&#x0A;- With more than one switch on, a client must pass all of them.&#x0A;- A cycle key is never dead: when no other client passes, the keys switch through all clients as if the switches were off, and a note near the cursor says so once.</sys:String>
