    <sys:String x:Key="L.Speech.Many">{0} clients: {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.sounds.speech">Spoken alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.speak">Speak alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.speak.Desc">A Windows voice says whose alert it is and what happened.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.speakFrom">Speak which alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.speakFrom.Desc">By severity: every event is critical, warning or info.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Opt.FromCritical">Critical only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Opt.FromWarning">Critical and warning</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Opt.FromInfo">All alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.speechVolume">Voice volume</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.speechVolume.Desc">How loud the voice is. Alert sounds have a volume of their own.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.voices">Voices</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.voices.Desc">The voice for each severity. Automatic takes a voice of MultiPreview's language when Windows has one.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.Automatic">Automatic: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.DefaultVoice">Windows' default voice</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.Try">Try</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.TryFor">Try the voice for {0} alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.VoiceFor">Voice for {0} alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.Example">This is how an alert sounds.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.NoVoice">Windows has no voice for MultiPreview's language. Alerts are spoken with another voice, which may read them badly. Add a voice in Windows' speech settings, or choose one here.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.NoVoices">Windows has no voice installed, so nothing can be spoken. Add one in Windows' speech settings.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.speech.Title">Spoken alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.speech.Body">With **Speak alerts** on, a Windows voice says whose alert it is and what happened: the character's name and the event's name, in MultiPreview's language. You know who needs you without looking, which matters most when many clients share one alert sound.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Speak which alerts** chooses by severity: critical only, critical and warning, or all.&#x0A;- Alerts of one event that arrive together are one sentence. Two clients are both named, as in "Alpha, Bravo, Under attack"; three or more are counted, as in "3 clients: Under attack". The most severe alert is said first.&#x0A;- An alert that has waited more than 10 seconds is not said at all. The same alert on the same client is not said again within 30 seconds, or within that event's sound quiet time if that is longer.&#x0A;- Speech is a sound: do not disturb holds the voice as it holds the alert sounds. While **Critical alerts still play** is on, as it is at first, critical alerts are still spoken; turn it off for complete silence. A muted character or a snoozed alert is not spoken. It does not need **Play alert sounds**: the voice works without the tones.&#x0A;- **Voices** sets a voice for each severity. Automatic takes a voice of MultiPreview's language when Windows has one, and Windows' default voice otherwise. Voices are added in Windows' own speech settings.&#x0A;- When a critical alert has been repeated as often as set and you still have not looked, it is spoken once more.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.hub.windows">Windows notifications</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.notify">Show alerts as Windows notifications</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.notify.Desc">An alert also appears as a notification of Windows and stays in its Notification Center until you have looked at that client.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.notifyFrom">Notify for which alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.notifyFrom.Desc">By severity: every event is critical, warning or info.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Notify.Switch">Switch to {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Notify.Snooze">Snooze for 15 minutes</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.hub.windows.Title">Windows notifications</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.hub.windows.Body">With **Show alerts as Windows notifications** on, an alert also appears as a notification of Windows itself, with the character's name and the event. It stays in Windows' Notification Center, so you can see what happened while you were away from the PC.&#x0A;&#x0A;- The first button brings that client to the front, and so does a click on the notification. The second button snoozes that alert for that character for 15 minutes.&#x0A;- There is one notification for each character and event. A newer alert updates it and counts up, and it pops up again at most every 30 seconds.&#x0A;- An alert on the client in front gets no notification, and a client's notifications go away when you look at it. A muted character and a snoozed alert get none.&#x0A;- The notification is silent: MultiPreview plays its own alert sounds. MultiPreview's do not disturb holds the alert sounds and the voice; a notification still comes.&#x0A;- Windows has its own switch for notifications: in Windows' settings under System, then Notifications, for all programs and for EVE MultiPreview alone. While that is off nothing appears, and a line on the **Test notification** card says so. **Show a test notification** shows one right away, so you can see for yourself; its buttons do nothing.&#x0A;- Windows decides whether a notification pops up: with its own do not disturb on, or while a game runs in full screen, it goes to the Notification Center only. Windows keeps the newest 20, each for up to 12 hours after its last alert.&#x0A;- Windows can show a notification's text on the lock screen. If nobody should read there which character was attacked, turn that off in Windows' notification settings.&#x0A;- While this is on, MultiPreview keeps one entry in the Windows registry and one picture file in its settings folder, so that Windows knows the name and the icon to show. Switching it off removes the entry and MultiPreview's notifications. The notifications are also taken away when MultiPreview closes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlarge">Enlarge the thumbnail on an alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlarge.Desc">The thumbnail grows for a few seconds, so you see what is happening there without switching.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeFrom">Enlarge for which alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeFrom.Desc">By severity: every event is critical, warning or info.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargePercent">Enlarged size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargePercent.Desc">In percent of the thumbnail's own size. It is never larger than the monitor.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeSeconds">Stays enlarged for</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeSeconds.Desc">Seconds from the alert. It goes back at once when you switch to that client.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.look.enlarge.Title">Enlarging on an alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.look.enlarge.Body">With **Enlarge the thumbnail on an alert** on, the thumbnail of a client that raises an alert grows for a few seconds, so you can see what is happening there without switching to it.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Enlarge for which alerts** chooses by severity. **Enlarged size** and **Stays enlarged for** set how large and how long. A newer alert on that client keeps it enlarged longer.&#x0A;- It goes back by itself when the time is over, and at once when that client comes to the front or you mute that character's alerts in the thumbnail's menu.&#x0A;- It grows around its own middle, never larger than the monitor, and is moved back onto the screen where it would stick out. Your layout is not changed, and nothing of the enlargement is saved.&#x0A;- The part that grew does not take clicks: to switch to that client, click where the thumbnail usually is. A click on the part that grew reaches another thumbnail if one lies under it, and nothing otherwise, so a thumbnail that grows under the pointer never switches clients by accident.&#x0A;- It stands above the thumbnails beside it only while thumbnails are kept on top: with **Always on top**, or with **Keep above EVE clients** while an EVE client is in front. Otherwise it grows where it stands among the other windows.&#x0A;- It shows the thumbnail's picture, larger. For a minimised client, and with **Still thumbnails**, that is the last snapshot, not what is happening at this moment.&#x0A;- The thumbnail under the pointer, one you are dragging and the client in front are not enlarged, and none grows while a thumbnail's right-click menu is open: it grows when the menu has closed. Second thumbnails and crop windows never are.&#x0A;- Do not disturb holds the alert sounds and the voice; the thumbnail still grows.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.events.groups">Alerts per group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.groups">Alerts for one group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.groups.Desc">A group can have its own choice of which events are on. An event left as everyone follows the table above.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Group">Group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.AsEveryoneOn">As everyone (on)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.AsEveryoneOff">As everyone (off)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.On">On for this group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Off">Off for this group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.ChoiceFor">{0} for the group {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Members">Members: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.NoMembers">This group has no members in this profile, so its choices do nothing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Overridden">Members who are in another group as well do not all get what the rows above say. When a character's groups disagree about an event, on wins: the event is on for that character. A group that left the event as everyone has no say.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.NoGroups">There are no groups yet. Make one on the Groups page.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Clear">Clear this group's choices</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.groups.Title">Alerts per group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.groups.Body">A group (Clients › Groups) can have its own choice of which events are on, so that the miners are told of a full cargo hold and the combat pilots are not.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Pick the group, then set an event to on or off for it. An event left as everyone follows its switch in **Alert events** above.&#x0A;- A group can switch on an event that is off for everyone, and switch off one that is on.&#x0A;- A character in no group always has the switches for everyone.&#x0A;- For a character in several groups, on wins: when its groups disagree about an event, the event is on. A group that left the event as everyone has no say. Under its rows, the card lists each member who gets something other than the rows say, with a group that says so.&#x0A;- An event that is off for a character is no alert for it: nothing is shown, played or spoken, and it is not in the alert history.&#x0A;- A custom rule can be switched off for a group. A rule that is switched off on its own page is off for everybody.&#x0A;- A group's choices go with it when you rename it, and do nothing while it has no members.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.neutralized">Capacitor neutralized</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.jammed">Jammed</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.ewarNote">Capacitor neutralized and Jammed are off until you switch them on, and are read from an English game client only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.ewar.Title">Capacitor neutralized and Jammed</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.ewar.Body">Two events for what often comes before the damage: **Capacitor neutralized**, when an energy neutralizer takes capacitor from your ship, and **Jammed**, when your ship is jammed and loses its target locks.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Both are off until you switch them on in the table above.&#x0A;- Each is raised once when it starts, then at most every 30 seconds for that character while it goes on.&#x0A;- They are read from the wording of an English game client. With the game in another language they raise nothing.&#x0A;- With **PVE mode** on, an NPC that neutralizes or jams you raises nothing, and neither does a player whose name the log line shows without a corporation ticker and without a ship type.&#x0A;- A nosferatu that drains your capacitor is not covered, only a neutralizer.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.TryFailed">Windows could not say that. Check that a voice is installed and that a sound device is connected.</sys:String>
